                            TERMIN  -  VERSION 3B

     Version 3 of the TERMIN program has a major new feature: it can be con-
figured at run time for a wide variety of different systems.  Features such
as the device code for the Input/Output ports, the baud rate and the type of
interface can all be specified when the program is called.  Furthermore, any
changes made can be saved, allowing a single distribution copy of the program
to be easily 'customized' for different installations.

     The desired options are specified on the command line that loads the pro-
gram into memory.  A 'VMS-like' syntax is employed to make the various parame-
ters easier to remember.  Thus the /SPEED option selects the desired baud rate,
while the /PERMANENT option saves all configuration changes so that they need
not be re-specified each time.  The following options are recognized:

     /NORMAL=xx       -  Specifies a 'normal' interface: KL8E, KL8JA, etc.
     /DECSTATION[=2]  -  Specifies a VT/WT/78 or VT/278 (SLU3[2]@9600 Bd)
     /SPEED=nnnn      -  Specifies Baud rate: 50-19200 (DECSTATIONS only!)
     /BAUD_RATE=nnnn  -  Same as '/SPEED=nnnn' (see baud rate list below)
     /INPUT=xx        -  Specifies the Input Device Code (01-77 octal)
     /OUTPUT=xx       -  Specifies the Output Device Code (same as above)
     /ARROW           -  Prints Control Characters as '^' + 'character'
     /HELP            -  Prints a summary of the command line options
     /VERSION         -  Prints the Version number of the program
     /PERMANENT       -  Updates the load file, saving specified options

     Example:  .R TERMIN /NORMAL=46 /PERMANENT

     This call runs the TERMIN program, specifying a 'standard' interface which
uses Device Codes 46 (for Input) and 47 (for Output).  The copy of TERMIN.SV on
the system device is updated to save the changes so that the next time the pro-
gram is used a '.R TERMIN' command will be sufficient.  (Note: the file date is
not changed and the program is updated on the device from which it was loaded.)

     Options may be specified in any order; the only rule is that the '/' must
be immediately followed by the first letter of the option word, and similarly,
the '=' sign must be immediately followed by a numerical parameter.  Options
are commonly abbreviated to their first letter: /I=34 is the same as /INPUT=34.

     Notes: (1) the Device Code specified by the /NORMAL option is that of the
Input Port; the Output Port is automatically set to the next higher value.  The
usual range for this parameter is 30-67, but no checking is performed.  Remem-
ber that device codes 20-27 are used for extended memory addressing.

     (2) The default for the /DECSTATION option is 'SLU3', which is the same as
the printer port on a '278.  To use 'SLU2' specify '/D=2'.  The default rate is
9600 baud; to pick a different rate (for example, 300), use: '.TER/D/S=300/P'.
Any of the following baud rates may be selected:  50, 75, 110, 134.5, 150, 300,
600, 1200, 1800, 2000, 2400, 3600, 4800, 7200, 9600, 19200.

     (3) The following error messages may appear on the console, followed by an
immmediate return to the Keyboard Monitor:

     NOT UNDER BATCH!          -  TERMIN is interactive, so no BATCH calls!
     INPUT DVC = OUTPUT DVC    -  Different I/O device codes are required
     BAD SPEED OR BAUD RATE    -  Value specified is not available (see list)
     WRITE ERROR ON LOAD DEV   -  An error occured while updating the program
                                TERMIN.SV

     The TERMIN program connects the console keyboard/display device to the
input/output ports of a communications interface (KL8E, KL8JA, SLU3, etc.).
This allows the user to interact with a remote computer system just as though
the terminal were connected directly to the communications line.  The program
is started by a '.R TERMIN' command, or more simply by just typing '.TER' if
you have the OS/78 version of CCL.  The following features are available:

     1) The baud rate of the communications line may be different from that
     of the terminal.  The program buffers all characters received from the
     communications line and automatically sends 'XOFF' when the buffer is
     nearly full (about 2000 characters).  A 'XON' is sent when the buffer
     is empty again.

     2) Typing CTRL/S on the keyboard stops output from the buffer and also
     sends XOFF to the remote system.  Typing CTRL/Q restarts output from
     the buffer and also sends XON to the remote system.  Typing RUBOUT (or
     DELETE) while output is stopped causes the buffer to be purged.  This
     feature is for slow hardcopy terminals which wish to simply 'dump' un-
     wanted output without stopping the program which is producing it.

     3) All keyboard characters are transmitted exactly as typed with two
     exceptions: CTRL/B causes a BREAK to be transmitted (KL8E, VT/78, '278
     only!), and CTRL/E causes an EXIT from the program back to OS/8.  Note
     that CTRL/C is not trapped and hence may be used to interrupt programs
     on the remote system.  Typing either CTRL/C or CTRL/Y will purge the
     output buffer (as will CTRL/S followed by DELETE as mentioned above).

     4) All printing characters (ASCII values 40-177) received from the re-
     mote system are transmitted to the terminal without change, as are the
     characters 'backspace', 'horizontal tab', 'linefeed', 'vertical tab',
     'formfeed', 'carriage return', 'bell' and 'escape'.  If the /ARROW op-
     tion is selected, all other control characters are printed as '^char'.

     5) A 'break' followed by CTRL/X is sent to the remote system when the
     program is first started.  This stops operation of the TU58 simulator
     in case it has control of the communications line.  A less desirable
     side-effect of this feature is that it causes the VAX to respond with
     a meaningless error message (message number 00000000) every time the
     TERMIN program is reloaded after the user has once logged in.

     6) The TERMIN program may be restarted (after a CTRL/E exit) by typing
     '.ST'.  The program fits entirely on Page 0, but uses 200-4000+ for the
     output buffer; the starting address is location 20.  The program may be
     run while a symbiont program is also in operation (VT/78 systems only).
     The baud rate used on VT/78's and 278's is set by the value in location
     177: '5'=300, '7'=1200, '16'=9600, etc. - see explanation of IOT '6333'.

|  TERMIN V3B has the following improvements: (1) the 'arrow flag' on control
|  characters is suppressed unless the ?ARROW switch is set.  This allows the
|  use of TERMIN with Tektronix terminals; (2) the /P (permanent) option will
|  now update the program using the device and program name specified, rather
|  than always assuming SYS:TERMIN.SV.  This allows the program to be renamed
|  'HOST.SV', for example, and still have the command .RU RXA1:HOST/A/P work.
|  (3) the /HELP option was added for those who use the program infrequently.

